{
  "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
  "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "R. TED CRUZ",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-30d35aea1449-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case concerns the continuing vitality of the procedural default rule, an important bulwark to federalism, comity, and finality of judgments."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-30d35aea1449-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, then why are you here? Don't -\u00ad is there some rule that you can't confess error in your state or?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-30d35aea1449-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, let me ask you this. Why -- why isn't there still an inadequate assistance of counsel claim out there, and why shouldn't the court address that before it gets into the question that it dealt with?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "R. TED CRUZ",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-30d35aea1449-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If it's sent back, we would be prepared to address the ineffective assistance claim on its merits."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-30d35aea1449-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: May I ask this question? I always thought there was a manifest injustice exception to the procedural default rule, and as I understand it, the Government concedes there was injustice at the end of their brief."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MATTHEW D. ROBERTS",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-30d35aea1449-0000-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The actual innocence exception applies in the extraordinary case where a prisoner has been convicted of a crime that he did not commit. The Court shouldn't extend that narrow exception to the very different situation where the prisoner is guilty of the crime of which he was convicted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-30d35aea1449-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Why not go to that first and tell them to do that, because this is just very difficult, that that would be the reason?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Roberts",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-30d35aea1449-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ROBERTS: Well, the -- the Court certainly could do that. There is a danger I think in -- in addressing the actual innocence exception in a case where there's cause and prejudice and so you don't need to address it, and in a case where the error is -- is clear when what the real concern is when it's not that clear about whether somebody's actually innocent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-30d35aea1449-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Why not? You think that counsel's performance was adequate here?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Albritton",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-30d35aea1449-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ALBRITTON: I absolutely would not, Your Honor. As we've argued in our brief -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1824",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "30d35aea144900fe51befe194be16542ccdc64dcad0a0319907cc65ae814bddf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-30d35aea1449-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "knew you were saying that, but I think Justice Scalia's question was that he can't find, nor can I find, a logical basis for making that distinction."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
